 "circuits": [],
 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Light to current",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "At VCE = 5 V the photocurrent follows a straight line on log-log axes, from about 20 µA at 10 lx to about 1.8 mA at 1000 lx. Above about 0.3 V the current climbs only gently with VCE; at 200 lx it goes from about 290 µA near 0.3 V to about 375 µA at 5 V. Saturation voltage is 0.1 V typ at 20 lx with the current held to 1.2 µA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Production spread and bins",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "At 20 lx the photocurrent spans 15 to 70 µA across production. Parts are sorted into three overlapping groups: A from 15 to 28.4 µA, B from 23.5 to 44.6 µA, C from 36.9 to 70 µA. Each bag carries one group; the group cannot be ordered and shipments mix groups."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Spectral and angular response",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Response follows the eye's curve: half response at 440 and 800 nm, peak at 570 nm, about a tenth of the peak near 360 nm and near 960 nm, and almost none by 1100 nm. Off axis, sensitivity is highest at about ±15° and dips to about 0.73 straight ahead, falling to half at ±30° and to nearly zero beyond ±45°."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Temperature",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Dark current climbs by nearly six decades over the range, from under 1 pA at -30 °C to about 0.25 µA at 90 °C (VCE = 5 V). Photocurrent under a white LED also rises with temperature: about 0.4 of its 25 °C value at -40 °C and about 2.1 times at 90 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power derating",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "With RthJA = 300 K/W the dissipation limit is 100 mW up to about 70 °C ambient, then falls along a straight line to about 49 mW at 85 °C, the top of the operating range. The absolute maximum rating states 100 mW for Tamb ≤ 55 °C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Capacitance",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Collector-emitter capacitance in the dark is 16 pF typ at 0 V and 1 MHz; across 0.1 to 6 V it stays between about 21 and 15 pF, falling slowly as VCE rises."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Lead soldering temperature is limited to 260 °C for no more than 3 s."
  }
 ],
